  "nl_text": "The database schema named 'schemabench_0056' contains four tables: 'abilities', 'assigned_roles', 'permissions', and 'roles'.\nThe 'abilities' table represents the abilities that users can have. It has columns for 'id', 'name', 'title', 'entity_id', 'entity_type', 'only_owned', 'options', 'scope', 'created_at', and 'updated_at'. These columns all allow null values except for 'id' and 'name', which are not null. 'options' is of JSON type.\nThe 'assigned_roles' table represents the roles assigned to entities. It has columns for 'id', 'role_id', 'entity_id', 'entity_type', 'restricted_to_id', 'restricted_to_type', and 'scope'. 'role_id' is a foreign key referencing the 'id' of the 'roles' table.\nThe 'permissions' table represents the permissions assigned to entities. It has columns for 'id', 'ability_id', 'entity_id', 'entity_type', 'forbidden', and 'scope'. 'ability_id' is a foreign key referencing the 'id' of the 'abilities' table.\nThe 'roles' table represents the roles that users can have. It has columns for 'id', 'name', 'title', 'level', 'scope', 'created_at', and 'updated_at'. These columns all allow null values except for 'id' and 'name', which are not null.\nEvery table has primary keys, which are used for entity integrity and referential integrity. For example, in the 'assigned_roles' table, 'role_id' references the 'id' of the 'roles' table, and in the 'permissions' table, 'ability_id' references the 'id' of the 'abilities' table. The 'CASCADE' option is used to automatically delete or update the records in the 'assigned_roles' and 'permissions' tables if the corresponding record in the 'roles' and 'abilities' tables is deleted or updated.\nThis schema is exhaustive and covers everything in the database schema.",
  "gold_question": "1) Background / Context:\nThe project focuses on creating a permissions and roles management system for applications built on the Laravel framework. The system leverages Eloquent models to define and manage roles and abilities through an authorization package named Bouncer. Bouncer is developed by Joseph Silber and operates with a framework-agnostic approach, allowing seamless integration with Laravel's authorization gates.\n\n2) Objectives:\nThe main objective is to provide a flexible, expressive way to manage user roles and permissions within Laravel applications. The system aims to stay unobtrusive, allowing developers to integrate custom code alongside Bouncer's capabilities.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users:\n- Developers: Implementing the role and permissions management system in Laravel applications.\n- Application Users: Individuals whose roles and abilities are defined using the system.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions:\n- Role Management: Define roles with associated abilities.\n- Ability Management: Create and manage abilities that can be attached to roles or users.\n- Authorization Gate: Use Bouncer to define authorization checks for different actions.\n- Integration Flexibility: Allows integration with existing app capabilities and precedence to custom code.\n- Database Integration: Utilizes Eloquent models to manage data related to roles, abilities, and permissions.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions:\n- Framework Agnostic: Designed to work primarily with Laravel but maintains flexibility for other frameworks.\n- Expressive Syntax: Facilitates an easy-to-understand configuration pathway for developers.\n- Performance: Efficiently handle authorization without significant impact on application performance.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_deepseek/0056.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0056\nCREATE TABLE `abilities` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `title` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `entity_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `entity_type` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `only_owned` tinyint(1) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `options` json DEFAULT NULL,\n  `scope` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `assigned_roles` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `role_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `entity_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `entity_type` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `restricted_to_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `restricted_to_type` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `scope` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `assigned_roles_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`role_id`) REFERENCES `roles` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `permissions` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `ability_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `entity_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `entity_type` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `forbidden` tinyint(1) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `scope` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `permissions_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`ability_id`) REFERENCES `abilities` (`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `roles` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `title` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `level` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `scope` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `created_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  `updated_at` datetime DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
